        "title": "Hydrodynamics and Free-Surface Flows",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Hydrodynamics is fluid dynamics with free surfaces (where water meets air), gravity waves, and flows dominated by gravity rather than pressure gradients. The key new feature: the free surface is a boundary that deforms with the flow. This creates waves, sloshing, hydraulic jumps, tsunamis, and geophysical circulation. The Froude number Fr = U/√(gL) is the governing parameter — it separates subcritical (Fr < 1, wave information propagates upstream) from supercritical (Fr > 1, hydraulic analogue of supersonic flow)."
    }
